  "body": "[Enqueue a custom element reaction](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/scripting.html#enqueue-a-custom-element-callback-reaction) has step 4 and this note:\n\n> Let definition be the entry in registry with name equal to element's local name.\n> Note: This algorithm can only be called when such a definition exists.\n\nHowever I don't think _definition_ is always defined and I think the note is wrong.\n\nIf a custom element is adopted by a document, then that document's window's custom elements registry may not have a definition that matches that element.\n\nIt's an interesting idea that an element would pick up the callbacks of the definition in the document it enters. _It is a bit odd that an element would pick up callbacks from a definition yet not run that definition's constructor._ What do you think about custom elements resetting their \"custom\" state on adoption and possibly entering another round of upgrading?\n\nBy the way,  in \"old\" custom elements, elements do not pick up definitions in their new document. Chrome uses custom elements internally to implement webview for Chrome extensions, and [this is a problem](https://crbug.com/282477) because the callbacks stop firing when the original window goes away (because of something roughly like [enqueue a custom element reaction](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/scripting.html#enqueue-a-custom-element-callback-reaction) step 2, which aborts if the document doesn't have a browsing context.) The built-in elements notably do not work this way.\n",
